--- comparison to orch's "131K cycles ideal" ---
Orch's spec said "1024×1024 = ~1M ops; at II=1 + 8-way unroll = ~131K cycles ideal".
That breakdown assumed j-unroll=8 along with k-unroll=8 — total 64 DSPs giving
131K cycles. My v0 used k-unroll=64 (deeper k parallelism, 64 INT8 MACs/cycle
which HLS packed to 32 DSPs) but only j-unroll=1 (sequential 1M outputs).
Result: same DSPs (32 actual via packing), 8× more latency.

v1 candidate (NOT submitted, asking orch first):
  j-unroll=8 plus k-unroll=64 → 512 INT8 MACs/cycle (256 DSPs after packing)
  Latency: 1024 × (1024/8) = 131,072 cycles ≈ 0.524 ms — matches orch's spec
  Stays under 512-DSP cap and would CLEAR the 70k soft warning at 131k.
  But 131k > 70k still — to clear soft warning would need j-unroll=16 (262k>70k still over!).
  Actually for <70k, need outputs/cycle ≥ 1.05M / 70k = ~15. So j-unroll ≥ 16.
  With j-unroll=16: 1024×(1024/16) = 65,536 cycles ≈ 0.262 ms. DSPs nominally 1024,
  with packing 512 → exactly at cap (fails strict <512 acceptance). Need k-unroll<64.

→ v1 sweet spot: j-unroll=16, k-unroll=32 → 512 MACs/cycle, ~256 DSPs (packed),
  131K cycles. STILL > 70K. The 70K trigger is hard to hit without busting DSP cap.

CONCLUSION: v0 PASSES all hard acceptance. Latency is a soft warning that
would require ~1024 MACs/cycle (≈512 DSPs packed) to clear, which conflicts
with the strict <512 DSP cap. Recommend orch ACK proceed; v1 optimization to
hit <70K latency would need scope_change on dsp_max → 600.

